1. Optimized Linux File System Layout – 50 GB Server
The 50 GB storage should be partitioned so that rapidly growing logs cannot consume the entire disk and affect critical services.
Proposed Partition Layout
Partition   	Size	  Purpose
/boot        	1 GB	  Kernel and boot files
/	            12 GB	  Operating system and applications
/var	        10 GB	  Logs, caches and variable data
/var/log	    10 GB	  System/application logs
/home	        8 GB	  User files
/opt	        5 GB	  Application data
Swap	        4 GB	  Virtual memory
Free/Reserved	0 GB	Managed through LVM
Since the requirement specifically states that logs must not exceed 10 GB, /var/log should be isolated as a separate partition or logical volume.
Storage Management
Use LVM (Logical Volume Manager) so that storage can be resized when required.
lsblk
df -h
sudo vgs
sudo lvs
Log Rotation
Configure logrotate to prevent unlimited log growth.
Example:
/var/log/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    maxsize 1G
}
This rotates logs daily and compresses old files.
Force rotation when required:
sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf
Monitoring
df -h
du -sh /var/log/*
Disk usage alerts can be configured when /var/log approaches 80–90%.
Advantages
Separate /var/log prevents logs from filling /.
LVM allows flexible storage management.
logrotate automatically limits log growth.
Compression saves disk space.
Monitoring detects storage problems early.
Critical services continue operating because the root filesystem retains free space.
Conclusion
A combination of separate partitions, LVM, logrotate, compression and disk monitoring ensures efficient use of the 50 GB storage while guaranteeing that logs remain within the 10 GB limit.

2. Kernel-Level Optimization for High CPU Workloads
The system experiences frequent context switching and must support real-time processing. Since hardware cannot be upgraded, kernel-level optimization should be used.
1. Analyze CPU and Context Switching
Use:
top
vmstat 1
pidstat -w 1
These tools identify high CPU usage and excessive context switching.

2. Scheduler Tuning
Linux scheduler behavior can be adjusted using sysctl.
For example:
sysctl -a | grep sched
CPU affinity can restrict a process to selected CPUs:
taskset -cp 2,3 <PID>
This reduces unnecessary CPU migration.

3. Process Priority
Use nice and renice:
sudo renice -n -5 -p <PID>
Important real-time workloads can receive higher priority.
For real-time scheduling:
chrt -f 50 <command>
SCHED_FIFO can provide predictable scheduling for appropriate real-time workloads.

4. CPU Isolation
Critical workloads can be assigned dedicated CPUs using kernel boot parameters such as:
isolcpus=2,3
This reduces interference from ordinary tasks.

5. Kernel Modules
Unnecessary kernel modules can be removed:
lsmod
sudo modprobe -r <module>
Required modules should be retained to avoid affecting hardware or services.

6. Monitor Improvements
After changes:
vmstat 1
pidstat -w 1
mpstat -P ALL 1
Compare context-switching and CPU utilization before and after optimization.
Advantages
Reduced unnecessary context switching.
Better CPU affinity.
Higher priority for real-time tasks.
Reduced interference between workloads.
No hardware upgrade required.
Conclusion
Combining scheduler tuning, CPU affinity, process priority, CPU isolation and careful kernel-module management can improve performance while maintaining real-time responsiveness.

3. Processing 1 Million Log Entries Using Shell Scripting
Processing 1 million entries within 2 minutes requires efficient commands and avoiding unnecessary loops and external processes.
Strategy
The main principle is:
Use Unix tools that process data in bulk rather than processing each line individually using a shell loop.
Efficient Commands
Assume the log file is:
access.log
Count entries:
wc -l access.log
Search errors:
grep -F "ERROR" access.log
Count error entries:
grep -Fc "ERROR" access.log
Extract fields:
awk '{print $1,$4,$7}' access.log
Sort and count repeated values:
awk '{print $7}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
Example Optimized Script
#!/bin/bash
LOG="access.log"
echo "Total entries:"
wc -l < "$LOG"
echo "Total errors:"
grep -Fc "ERROR" "$LOG"
echo "Top requested URLs:"
awk '{print $7}' "$LOG" |
sort |
uniq -c |
sort -nr |
head -10
Performance Optimization
Avoid:
while read line
do
done < access.log
for million-line processing when standard utilities can perform the operation directly.
Avoid repeatedly calling commands inside loops because each command creates an additional process.
Prefer:
awk
grep
sort
uniq
cut
wc
These utilities are optimized for large text streams.
Parallel Processing
If the analysis contains independent operations, they can be executed in parallel:
grep -Fc "ERROR" access.log &
awk '{print $7}' access.log | sort | uniq -c &
wait
Performance Measurement
time ./logprocess.sh
The script should be tested with representative 1-million-entry logs and optimized until execution remains below the required 2-minute limit.
Conclusion
Using bulk-processing commands, pipelines, fixed-string grep, AWK, avoiding shell loops and parallelizing independent operations provides an efficient solution for processing one million log entries.

4. Managing a Faulty Process Consuming 90% CPU
The objective is to identify and control the faulty process without rebooting the server and without affecting critical services.
Step 1 – Identify the Process
Use:
top
or:
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head
This identifies the process consuming excessive CPU.
Example:
PID    USER     %CPU    COMMAND
4521   user     90.0    faulty_app

Step 2 – Monitor the Process
top -p 4521
or:
pidstat -p 4521 1
Check whether CPU usage remains consistently high.

Step 3 – Identify Process Details
ps -fp 4521
Check its parent:
pstree -p 4521
This helps determine whether the process belongs to a critical service.

Step 4 – Reduce CPU Priority
If the process is non-critical:
sudo renice +10 -p 4521
A higher nice value reduces its scheduling priority.

Step 5 – Restrict CPU Usage
Using cpulimit where available:
sudo cpulimit -p 4521 -l 30
This can limit the process to approximately 30% CPU.
CPU affinity can also be changed:
sudo taskset -cp 0 4521

Step 6 – Graceful Termination
If the process continues causing problems:
sudo kill -TERM 4521
SIGTERM allows the application to shut down cleanly.
If it does not respond:
sudo kill -KILL 4521
SIGKILL should be the last resort.

Step 7 – Protect Critical Services
Before terminating the process:
systemctl status <critical-service>
Critical services should not be stopped or restarted unnecessarily.
Monitoring After Action
top
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head
Confirm that CPU utilization has returned to normal.
Conclusion
The appropriate strategy is:
Identify → Monitor → Verify importance → Lower priority/limit CPU → Gracefully terminate if necessary → Monitor again.
This solves the problem without rebooting and minimizes impact on critical services.

